implementationPlan.description:
THE INCIDENT LIFECYCLE. Create `apps/daemon/src/monitoring/health-incident.ts` (+ bridge).

EXACTLY ONE OPEN INCIDENT PER ENVIRONMENT OUTAGE:
- three CONSECUTIVE failures open one incident;
- continued failure opens NO second — the open incident is found and left alone;
- recovery CLOSES it;
- a later outage opens a NEW one, so closing is not the same as suppressing.

KEY BY ENVIRONMENT and check for an existing OPEN incident before opening. The alternative — opening per failing probe and de-duplicating at read time — was rejected at plan time: it makes the durable record itself the wrong shape and pushes the invariant onto every future consumer.

WHY THIS IS THE ROW'S OPERATOR-FACING PROPERTY: a per-probe raise floods the queue at one item a minute, precisely when it must stay usable. The incident is what a human sees during an outage; making it noisy makes it useless.

THE INCIDENT IS A DURABLE RECORD, NOT A CARD (rail 1). Write it to the store; a later UI row derives a card. Touch NO file under apps/control-room — Needs-you is a closed four-kind roster whose items route to a goal, a deployment outage does not, and adding a fifth kind forces two exhaustive Record maps in files two peer rows are already editing.

STATE THIS SEAM IN THE COMPLETION RECORD so the parent's DoD 1 ("Needs-you incident card") is not read as unmet: this row delivers the durable incident and names the row that will derive the card.

THE INCIDENT TEST (DoD 1) — `health-incident.test.ts`. This is the row's central arm and it must be driven as a SEQUENCE, not as three separate cases.

Run one continuous sequence against a REAL store and COUNT THE INCIDENTS AT EVERY STAGE:
1. two failures — assert ZERO incidents (the threshold is three, and an off-by-one here pages on every transient blip);
2. the third consecutive failure — assert EXACTLY ONE;
3. TEN MORE failures — assert STILL EXACTLY ONE. DoD 1 names ten specifically. An arm that checks only "an incident exists" after three failures passes for a loop that opens one per probe, and that loop is invisible until an outage floods the queue;
4. a recovery — assert the incident is CLOSED, and that the count of OPEN incidents is zero while the closed record survives;
5. a SECOND outage — assert a NEW incident opens. Closing must not mean suppressing, or the second outage is silent.

Count incidents in the DURABLE STORE across the whole sequence, not in a return value — the record is what a later UI row and the sibling rollback work read.

Also assert an intermittent pattern: fail, fail, succeed, fail, fail, fail. The counter must RESET on success, so this opens exactly one incident at the sixth probe and none earlier. A non-resetting counter passes the simple consecutive case and pages on unrelated blips forever.

MUTATE AND CONFIRM RED: remove the existing-open-incident check so it opens per failure, run the sequence, confirm stage 3 goes RED. Restore and prove it by SHA256 against an out-of-tree backup — this file is new and untracked.
